From: ReArc Authors
Subject: [PATCH] P-TAB-001 screen sharing: a tab nobody looks at is shared, and its page stays hidden (bridge 0.82.0)

The release self-test of Darni 2026.1124.1323, step "screen sharing": the
page asks, the person picks another tab in the sheet, the engine answers
"shared" and reports the share (media_capture, capturing_display true), and
ten seconds later reports it over. The page's getDisplayMedia() never
resolved. 1329 shipped with screen sharing off because of it.

Why, part one: no size. A bridge tab gets the size of its view from the
host's first attachment (attachment_lease.cc). A tab the host never showed
has a WebContentsView of 0 x 0: the bridge passes no initial size and
answers no WebContentsDelegate::GetSizeForNewRenderView. Its page is laid
out in a viewport of 0 x 0 and its compositor has nothing to draw.
FrameSinkVideoCaptureDevice tells its client that the capture has started
with the first frame only (OnFrameCaptured), a frame never comes, and the
asking renderer's VideoCaptureImpl gives up after kCaptureStartTimeout, ten
seconds: the stream's source fails, the request ends, the indicator goes.
Chrome does not meet this, because it sizes a tab when it is made (the
size of the tab it is opened from).

Seen on the release engine (out/ReArc-codecs-arm64, bridge 0.81.0, overlay
f4f542bf08) with the test host below in report mode: a tab never attached
reads innerWidth 0, innerHeight 0; its share is reported, and 10.2 seconds
later the page has "AbortError: Timeout starting video source". With the
tab's sound and without it alike: the sound is not the cause. A tab that
was attached once (400 x 300) and detached is live after 0.8 seconds, with
its sound.

Why, part two: what Chromium tells the shared page. A capture of a tab is a
visible capturer, twice: WebContentsFrameTracker (the picture) and
ForwardingAudioStreamFactory::LoopbackStreamStarted (the tab's sound) call
WebContents::IncrementCapturerCount with stay_hidden false, and
CalculatePageVisibilityState then answers kVisible for a tab in the
background. Its page gets document.visibilityState "visible" and a
visibilitychange event when the share starts, and "hidden" with another
event when it ends. Seen on the release engine (156.0.8078.114, the stock
launcher of out/ReArc-codecs-arm64, headless, with the tab switch of
Chrome's own sharing turned off by CaptureController): the page of the
shared tab in the background went hidden true -> false -> true, with two
events. The same on the release engine's bridge, with the test host: the
detached tab's page read hidden false while it was shared, with one more
visibilitychange, and its devicePixelRatio went from 2 to 4 (Chromium's
WebContentsAutoScaler raises a captured page's scale for a sharper
picture). A page could tell from either that it is being shared, and when.
The security review of this fix forbids it: only the capture's own frames
may change.

What this patch does.

- WebContentsDelegate::KeepsPageHiddenWhileCaptured (new, false by default:
  Chromium unchanged). WebContentsFrameTracker asks it when a capture
  starts, ForwardingAudioStreamFactory when the loopback of the tab's sound
  starts, and with true each counts as a hidden capturer
  (IncrementCapturerCount with stay_hidden true, and no preferred size; the
  closure is the matching decrement, as before). The first build of this
  patch had the picture only, and its test showed a tab shared with its
  sound still told it was visible. The page's state is then
  PageVisibilityState::kHiddenButPainting while nothing shows the tab: the
  widget is shown and draws frames for the capture, and Blink exposes that
  state to scripts exactly as kHidden (runtime_enabled_features.json5,
  DispatchHiddenVisibilityTransitions: "no visibilitychanged event fired
  when switching between them"). This is the state Chromium's own tab
  thumbnails use for a tab in the background. WebContents::GetVisibility
  is not touched: the tab is never marked visible. A tab that is on screen
  is visible for that reason alone, as before.
- Such a capture makes no WebContentsAutoScaler: the page's device scale
  factor stays what it is, shared or not. The picture of a small tab is
  then as sharp as the tab is drawn, and no sharper.
- ReArcTabDelegate answers true for every bridge tab.
- HostPicker::ShareTab gives a target with no size the size of the asking
  tab before it answers (GiveSizeToUnshownTab: WebContents::Resize). The
  host's first attachment sets the view's frame again, as for any tab. The
  page sees a resize from 0 x 0, as it does when it is first shown.
- Feature "display_capture_hidden_tab", bridge 0.82.0. The shell installs
  its display-capture callback only with it, so an engine without this
  patch keeps refusing every request at once (NotSupportedError) instead of
  failing some shares after ten seconds.

What a shared page can still tell. Its size changes if it had none. And
it is drawn: while it is shared in the background its animation frames run
(about 45 requestAnimationFrame callbacks in a second of sharing, in the
test), which they do not in a tab that is only hidden. That is the
capture's own frame production; Chromium's tab thumbnails do the same.

State. Nothing is persisted; no preference, no content setting.

No signature or struct changes; ABI 1.70 stays.

Tests: engine/tests/c_abi_display_capture_host.mm (run_c_abi.py
display_capture): five shares at the engine boundary, a tab never attached
with and without sound, a tab shown and detached with and without sound,
and the asking tab itself; each must be live with frames, and the shared
page's document.hidden, its count of visibilitychange events and its
devicePixelRatio must be the same before, during and after. Its window is
off every screen and it sends no input. REARC_CAPTURE_REPORT=1 prints what
an engine without the patch does instead; engine/tests/
headless_tab_capture.mjs shows Chromium's own capture on a stock launcher. The shell's self-test step
"screen sharing" (ChromiumSelfTest.screenShareChecks) checks the same for
a tab never shown and for the same tab once it was shown.

Type-checked with the tree's clang (out/ReArc-dev-arm64 flags, Chromium's
plugins, -fsyntax-only), then built in out/ReArc-codecs-arm64 on 2026-11-24:
overlay 08d13afdf2, and the sound's loopback as a second overlay commit,
86afa51467.

Run on that build (run_c_abi.py display_capture, REARC_CHROMIUM_OUT the
release out dir, nothing on screen): the five shares are live in 0.3 to 0.5
seconds with frames of 800 x 600; the two tabs never attached read 400 x 300
afterwards; every shared page in the background reads hidden true, the same
count of visibilitychange events and devicePixelRatio 2 before, during and
after its share; the network audit shows loopback only. Not run yet: the
shell's self-test step, which needs the screen.

Touches: content/public/browser/web_contents_delegate.{h,cc},
content/browser/media/capture/web_contents_frame_tracker.cc and
content/browser/media/forwarding_audio_stream_factory.cc (first ReArc hunk
in each), and the bridge's tab_delegate.{h,cc}, rearc_display_capture.cc,
rearc_engine_abi.{h,mm}, rearc_engine_entry_mac.mm.

Last rebased: 2026-11-24 onto Chromium 156.0.8078.114 (overlay f4f542bf08).

Co-Authored-By: Claude Opus 5.5 <noreply@anthropic.com>

